## 3. Maintenance Levels and Protection

| Level | Scope | Impact | Execution boundary |
|---|---|---|---|
| A: project caches | Confirmed regenerable test/bytecode/incremental caches | Regenerated by future checks/builds | Exact authorized paths, excluding source/backups |
| B: runtime reset | Debug databases/data under `local/` | Existing records lost; instances may be affected | Human operation; AI must not delete/reset existing `local/` |
| C: dependency/build recovery | Virtual environments, dependency directories, build outputs | Reinstall/rebuild required | Known recovery method; `local/` deletion remains human-only |
| D: shared caches | User-wide package/browser runtimes | Other projects may be affected | Explicit shared/global scope; never inferred from project maintenance |

- Captured `_adflow_backup/` snapshots are read-only and excluded from all levels.
- Verify path existence, live consumers and recovery. Do not recursively clean the whole project or hide errors while reporting success.
- Cleanup examples remain subject to these boundaries and grant no authority.
- Source, tests, configuration, lockfiles and build inputs are not caches. Do not reset data/dependencies to bypass an unexplained failure.

## 4. Maintenance and Recovery

1. **Inventory read-only:** identify stack, actual paths, disk use and runtime consumers; separate project assets, regenerable caches and shared resources.
2. **Handle scoped paths:** use the existing authorization and applicable level. If scope is unclear, continue independent inventory and report the unresolved dependency; do not repeat already granted permission questions.
3. **Verify impact:** record actual commands, exits and resulting state; check source/protected assets were retained and report failures.
4. **Recover as needed:** use actual manifests/lockfiles and relevant tests or health checks. Report network, credential and manual-operation gaps; examples do not prove recovery.

Use the existing C/T card or report for maintenance evidence. Do not introduce another approval form, state or mandatory schedule.
